<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karakoram Highway</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as KKH</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Karakoram Highway is a road that runs from Hasan Abdal in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> through the Karakoram range and over the Khunjerab Pass to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Built by Pakistan and China between 1959 and 1979, it is one of the highest paved roads in the world. It passes through the Hunza valley and opened the region to trade and tourism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karakoram-highway.jpg" alt="Karakoram Highway" width="320" height="458" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: de:Benutzer:Grag, Tevatron, Lexicon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KKH.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Karakoram Highway, also called the KKH, National Highway 35 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>–Pakistan Friendship Highway, was built jointly by the governments of China and Pakistan. It was completed in 1979 and opened to the public in 1986. Pakistan at first favoured a route through the Mintaka Pass, but in 1966 China recommended the steeper Khunjerab Pass instead, noting that Mintaka would be more exposed to air strikes. On the Pakistani side the road was built by the Frontier Works Organisation, using the Pakistan Army Corps of Engineers.</p><p>The work was costly in lives: about 810 Pakistanis and about 200 Chinese workers died, mostly in landslides and falls. More than 140 of the Chinese dead are buried in the Chinese cemetery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgit">Gilgit</a>. The route follows one of the many paths of the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The highway runs about 1,300 km. Its Pakistani section is 806 km long and meets the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus River</a> at Thakot, then follows it to Jaglot, where the Gilgit River joins it. There the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a> and the Karakoram meet. The road passes through Gilgit, the capital of Gilgit–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltistan">Baltistan</a>, and the valleys of Nagar and Hunza, and reaches the Chinese border at the Khunjerab Pass, at a maximum elevation of 4,714 m. It is one of the highest paved roads in the world.</p><p>On the Chinese side the road follows the Sarykol valley west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a>, enters the canyon of the Gez River and passes Karakul Lake and the peak of Muztagh Ata before descending to Tashkurgan. The highway crosses the collision zone between the Eurasian and Indian plates and has strategic importance for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The highway gives access to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgit">Gilgit</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skardu">Skardu</a>, the hubs for mountaineering in Gilgit–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltistan">Baltistan</a>, and to glaciers such as the Passu and Minapin. More than 50,000 rock carvings stand along it, concentrated at ten major sites between Hunza and Shatial; the earliest are dated to between 5000 and 1000 BCE. Daily bus service between Gilgit and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a> began on 1 June 2006.</p><p>In January 2010 a massive landslide above Karimabad in the Hunza Valley created Attabad Lake, which flooded more than 20 km of the highway and cut through traffic to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Goods crossed the lake by boat until a new route with five tunnels and several bridges, built by the China Road &amp; Bridge Corporation from 2012, was inaugurated on 15 September 2015.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karakoram Highway” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Karakoram Highway</b> now runs through the valley, and Baltit Fort is a museum.</blockquote><a href="/state/hunza-state">Hunza State</a></li></ul></section>
